     "title": "BAE Systems advances to Phase 2 of DARPA’s THREADS program",
     "url": "https://www.semiconductor-today.com/news_items/2026/aug/baesystems-130826.shtml",
     "time": "08-13 15:32",
     "ts": 1786606368,
     "summary": "BAE Systems Inc says that its FAST Labs research, development and production organization of Nashua, NH, USA has completed Phase 1 of the US Defense Advanced Re",
     "source": "Semiconductor Today",
     "content": "News: Microelectronics\n13 August 2026\nBAE Systems advances to Phase 2 of DARPA’s THREADS program\nBAE Systems Inc says that its FAST Labs research, development and production organization of Nashua, NH, USA has completed Phase 1 of the US Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency’s (DARPA) program Technologies for Heat Removal in Electronics at the Device Scale (THREADS) and has been awarded continued support to move into Phase 2.\nThe THREADS program aims to overcome temperature limitations in high-performance radio frequency (RF) electronics, specifically in gallium nitride (GaN) devices. Overcoming these limitations will significantly increase the power and range of vital military systems.\n“The progress we’ve made during Phase 1 validates our approach to material and process enhancements and brings us closer to unlocking the full potential of RF-based systems for our warfighters,” says Isaac Wildeson, principal investigator at BAE Systems’ FAST Labs.\nSuccessful thermal management developed through this program is expected to nearly triple the range of RF systems, enhancing the safety and engagement distances for military personnel. BAE Systems’ work on THREADS is being conducted at its Microelectronics Center (MEC) in Nashua, NH, USA, a Department of War (DoW) Category 1A Trusted Supplier, leveraging its established expertise in the development and manufacturing of GaN and gallium arsenide integrated circuits.\nTo accelerate the development of this critical technology, BAE Systems’ work on the THREADS program includes collaboration with Modern Microsystems, Penn State University, Stanford University, the University of Notre Dame, and the University of Texas at Dallas.\nBAE Systems awarded $12m THREADS contract to boost effectiveness of RF-based applications",
     "_extract_method": "trafilatura",
     "zh": "BAE系统公司推进至DARPA THREADS项目第二阶段",
     "content_zh": "2026年8月13日微电子领域消息，BAE系统公司表示其位于美国新罕布什尔州纳舒厄的FAST Labs研发生产机构已完成美国国防高级研究计划局（DARPA）“器件级电子设备散热技术（THREADS）”项目第一阶段，获准进入第二阶段并获得持续支持。该项目旨在突破高性能射频电子设备尤其是氮化镓器件的温度限制，突破后可显著提升关键军事系统的功率和覆盖范围。项目研发的成功热管理方案预计可将射频系统覆盖范围提升近三倍，增强军事人员的安全性和交战距离。"
