     "title": "Ford’s $28,000 Fathom EV nears production after $2 billion factory overhaul",
     "url": "https://www.theverge.com/transportation/979565/ford-fathom-uev-louisville-assembly-plant",
     "time": "08-14 01:23",
     "ts": 1786641834,
     "summary": "Ford said today that its next-generation electric vehicle - recently dubbed Fathom - will go into production at the automaker's recently overhauled Louisville A",
     "source": "The Verge Transport",
     "content": "Ford said today that its next-generation electric vehicle — recently dubbed Fathom — will go into production at the automaker’s recently overhauled Louisville Assembly Plant in the first quarter of 2027.\nFord’s $28,000 Fathom EV nears production after $2 billion factory overhaul\nThe new electric truck will go into production at Ford’s Louisville factory in the first quarter of 2027.\nThe new electric truck will go into production at Ford’s Louisville factory in the first quarter of 2027.\nThe first Fathoms will be prototypes, with Ford’s team in Louisville already in the production-level pre-tooling phase at the recently converted facility. Factory workers are working alongside team’s at Ford’s New Model Product Development Center in Dearborn, Michigan, hand-building the Fathom prototypes — and the same operators who helped design the high-stakes EV will also be involved in its production.\nThe factory is over 70 years old, so Ford had a lot of equipment to get rid of before it could begin the process of converting into an EV plant. Over several weeks, crews physically unbolted and hauled out heavy machinery, dragging thousands of tons of steel out the door. By February, only an empty shell remained.\nThe Fathom, a $28,000 electric pickup truck, will be the first vehicle on Ford’s new Universal Electric Vehicle Platform, an innovative new system designed to maximize aerodynamic range while also reducing production costs so Ford can make a profitable vehicle. For over a year, the company has been hyping it as its “Model T moment” that will revolutionize nearly everything at the 123-year-old company.\nFord said it converted the plant from one that makes gas cars, like the Escape and the Lincoln Corsair, to an EV only plant in just under a year. In total, Ford is spending $5 billion to create 4,000 new jobs at both the Louisville factory and its BlueOval Battery Park in Michigan.\nEven the production process will be totally different. Rather than one long conveyor, Ford is using a new assembly tree process that splits the vehicle into three sections — front clip, rear clip, and battery deck — which enables better access so more operators can work on the vehicle at the same time. Ford will use large unicasting to create the front and rear sections, replacing dozens of stamped and welded parts as it currently uses. Ford says it can speed up the production process by up to 15 percent by using the assembly tree.\nTo illustrate how much more efficient this new process will be, Ford cites this example:\nOne example of that efficiency in action is the software module flash station, a first for a Ford assembly plant, which ensures vehicles have the latest software prior to module installation. Previously, this software flash happened after the vehicle was built, and if issues were detected, updates took 10 minutes per vehicle. With private 5G in the plant, operators can perform quality tests like these as a vehicle rolls down the assembly tree\nFord is banking on th",
     "_extract_method": "trafilatura",
     "zh": "福特2.8万美元Fathom电动皮卡完成20亿美元工厂改造后即将投产",
     "content_zh": "福特今日宣布，其最新一代命名为Fathom的电动皮卡将于2027年第一季度在刚完成20亿美元改造的路易斯维尔组装工厂投产。目前首批Fathom原型车已进入生产级预调试阶段，福特路易斯维尔工厂团队与密歇根州迪尔伯恩新车型产品开发中心的团队协作手工打造原型车，参与设计该核心电动车型的工人也将参与后续量产工作。该工厂已有70余年历史，改造过程中工人耗时数周拆除大量旧设备，运出了数千吨钢材。"
