     "summary": "US 'multi-day' energy storage startup Noon Energy has made an agreement with renewables platform Sabanci Renewables, to deploy 1GW of Noon’s technology for AI i",
     "source": "Energy Storage News",
     "content": "US ‘multi-day’ energy storage startup Noon Energy has made an agreement with renewables platform Sabanci Renewables to deploy 1GW of Noon’s technology for AI infrastructure in the US.\nAnnounced 13 August, the companies will co-develop ultra-long-duration energy storage (LDES) projects, integrating Noon Energy’s reversible electrofuels battery energy storage systems (BESS) with Sabanci’s renewables portfolio “to deliver 100% clean, firm power for AI infrastructure.”\nThis collaboration targets up to 1GW of capacity. Noon noted that sister company of Sabanci Renewables and Noon Energy investor Sabanci Climate Ventures enabled the collaboration.\nThe projects developed through the agreement will be structured as power purchase agreements (PPAs) or capacity offtake agreements for systems up to 1GW.\nTry Premium for just $1\n- Full premium access for the first month at only $1\n- Converts to an annual rate after 30 days unless cancelled\n- Cancel anytime during the trial period\nPremium Benefits\n- Expert industry analysis and interviews\n- Digital access to PV Tech Power journal\n- Exclusive event discounts\nOr get the full Premium subscription right away\nOr continue reading this article for free\nThis will also assist with Sabanci Renewables’ goal of having a 3GW renewable portfolio in the US in the next five years.\nIn April, Noon announced an agreement with Meta to reserve up to 1GW/100GWh of LDES capacity, beginning with a 25MW/2.5GWh project, scheduled for completion by 2028.\nFollowing the success of the 2.5GWh project, Noon will begin delivering systems under the 100GWh supply contract.\nNoon Energy’s battery operates on principles similar to flow batteries, storing energy in liquid electrolyte tanks separated from the power stack. Like flow batteries, the Noon fuel cell system allows power and energy to be decoupled.\nThe system comprises three primary components: a power block utilising reversible solid oxide fuel cell technology to convert electricity into stored energy; a charge tank that transforms electricity into a carbon-based storage medium while releasing oxygen; and a discharge tank that draws oxygen from the air to convert stored energy back into electricity.\nIn January, Noon unveiled its first operational demonstration project. Though the company provided limited technical specifications—such as power output capacity—it claimed the system can discharge for 200 hours at maximum output.\nESN Premium’s March analysis compared 100-hour LDES batteries from Form, Noon, and Ore Energy. All three can discharge for at least 100 hours, with Noon claiming over 200 hours, though this remains unproven in operational settings.\nIron-air batteries can fully discharge to 0% state of charge (SOC) without damage due to their reversible rusting process, unlike lithium-ion batteries that degrade at extreme charge levels. Noon Energy hasn’t revealed its deep discharge capabilities, which are likely inferior to iron-air systems.\nBecause Noon’s technology relies on solid",
