     "summary": "Origin Energy has confirmed that its 300MW/650MWh Mortlake BESS in south-west Victoria has commenced commercial operations.",
     "source": "Energy Storage News",
     "content": "Origin Energy has confirmed that its 300MW/650MWh Mortlake battery energy storage system (BESS) in south-west Victoria has commenced commercial operations, following completion of major commissioning works.\nOrigin Energy is one of Australia’s largest energy companies, operating as both a generator and retailer across electricity, gas and renewable energy.\nThe Mortlake facility is located at the site of Origin’s existing Mortlake Power Station, around 200km west of Melbourne, and connects directly to AusNet’s 500kV switchyard through a dedicated substation.\nThe AU$400 million (US$279 million) project was first announced by Origin in January 2024, with construction beginning that August. Fluence served as the main contractor for the build.\nTry Premium for just $1\n- Full premium access for the first month at only $1\n- Converts to an annual rate after 30 days unless cancelled\n- Cancel anytime during the trial period\nPremium Benefits\n- Expert industry analysis and interviews\n- Digital access to PV Tech Power journal\n- Exclusive event discounts\nOr get the full Premium subscription right away\nOr continue reading this article for free\nThe facility uses grid-forming inverter technology, distinguishing it from conventional grid-following battery systems through its ability to set its own internal voltage waveform reference and operate independently of other generation sources when required, allowing it to actively stabilise the grid in a manner similar to synchronous generators.\nHowever, it should be noted that grid-forming inverter-equipped battery storage systems have not yet been confirmed to meet the highest tier of system strength requirements in the NEM, according to the Australian Energy Market Operator (AEMO).\nThis hasn’t stopped the technology from becoming a core pillar of battery storage systems in Australia, with grid-forming inverters featuring in 74% of Australia’s 33.2GW NEM battery storage pipeline.\nFrom commissioning to commercial operation\nThe commercial launch follows a formal testing process that began in early 2026. The system, designated MLB01 in the AEMO registry, entered AEMO’s Market Management System in March 2026, marking its transition into low-load injection and consumption testing to verify integration with Victoria’s transmission network.\nThat step followed Origin’s completion of unconditional connection agreements with AEMO in November 2025, which provided the regulatory certainty needed to proceed through the project’s final construction phases after satisfying the technical assessments and grid-impact studies required for safe integration.\nMortlake’s location also adds to a growing concentration of grid-scale battery storage in western Victoria, sitting roughly 30km northwest of the 100MW/200MWh Terang BESS.\nThe clustering of large battery storage systems in the region reflects both the availability of established transmission infrastructure at legacy generation sites and the value grid-forming assets provide in areas of t",
