     "source": "PV Tech",
     "content": "Actis-backed Rezolv Energy has secured the final legal approval required to begin construction of its 1.2GW Dama Solar project in Arad County in the west of Romania.\nThe Romanian Energy Regulatory Authority (ANRE) has issued the solar PV project’s setting-up authorisation, following an increase in planned capacity from earlier designs through technological advances and optimisation.\nTry Premium for just $1\n- Full premium access for the first month at only $1\n- Converts to an annual rate after 30 days unless cancelled\n- Cancel anytime during the trial period\nPremium Benefits\n- Expert industry analysis and interviews\n- Digital access to PV Tech Power journal\n- Exclusive event discounts\nOr get the full Premium subscription right away\nOr continue reading this article for free\nLocated across more than 1,064 hectares in the Grăniceri and Pilu communes, Dama Solar is aiming to begin commercial operation in 2028. According to the firm, the project is set to become the largest solar PV plant in the European Union (EU) once commissioned. Construction is expected to begin after financial close.\nThe project is backed by two contracts for difference (CfDs) awarded by Romania’s Ministry of Energy in the country’s second CfD auction in 2025. Rezolv also plans to install a large-scale battery energy storage system (BESS) alongside the solar plant.\nThe project will include an 82-hectare nature reserve, which Rezolv said would be one of the largest private-led nature restoration initiatives associated with a solar project in south-east Europe.\nRezolv is currently progressing financing and procurement and expects to reach financial close soon, the company said.\nThe project was acquired from the Monsson Group in 2022, with the company then expecting it to feature 1.6 million solar panels and generate an average 1,500GWh of electricity annually.\nRezolv Energy was launched by Actis in July 2022, with co-investment from Mubadala, to develop renewable energy projects across central and south-east Europe. Headquartered in Prague, the company has a portfolio spanning solar and wind, including the 225MW St. George Solar Park in Bulgaria and two wind projects in Romania.\nEconergy connects 59MW Iancu Jianu solar project in Romania\nIn other Romanian news, Israel-headquartered independent power producer (IPP) Econergy Renewable Energy has connected its 59MW Iancu Jianu solar PV project to the country’s electricity grid.\nThe project is Econergy’s fifth connected project in Romania, taking its connected and ready-to-connect capacity in the country to 743MW, alongside a further 861MW under construction. The company plans to gradually ramp-up power deliveries.\nEconergy is also developing a 35MW/70MWh BESS at Iancu Jianu, which is expected to connect to the grid in the second quarter of 2027.\nThe BESS is part of Econergy’s broader strategy to co-locate storage with solar projects, targeting additional revenues from grid balancing, price arbitrage and peak-demand response.\nEconergy ",
