     "title": "Comstock, OnePlanet reach testing and engineering milestones on US solar recycling lines",
     "url": "https://www.pv-tech.org/comstock-oneplanet-reach-testing-and-engineering-milestones-on-us-solar-recycling-lines/",
     "time": "08-13 20:04",
     "ts": 1786622683,
     "summary": "Comstock has advanced its industry-scale PV recycling line, while Florida-based OnePlanet has launched its 'PRISM' system to recover valuable materials from end",
     "source": "PV Tech",
     "content": "US recycling solutions firm Comstock Metals has fully integrated and tested its solar panel recycling system, moving its first industry-scale facility into the final stages of commissioning and production ramp-up.\nThe company said all major processing stages have now been connected and successfully operated, completing testing of the facility’s individual unit operations.\nTry Premium for just $1\n- Full premium access for the first month at only $1\n- Converts to an annual rate after 30 days unless cancelled\n- Cancel anytime during the trial period\nPremium Benefits\n- Expert industry analysis and interviews\n- Digital access to PV Tech Power journal\n- Exclusive event discounts\nOr get the full Premium subscription right away\nOr continue reading this article for free\nFinal integration is expected to be completed in mid-August, after which Comstock Metals will begin ramping up towards its first production milestone, the company said.\nFortunato Villamagna, president of Comstock Metals said: “We are now leveraging the modular nature of the start-up process to train and develop our operating crews as we transition from a 24-hour, five-day-per-week schedule to a 24/7 schedule using 12-hour rotating shifts. These activities remain on schedule through August as we ramp toward our first production milestone of continuously operating at 25% of rated capacity.”\nThe facility, designed with an annual processing capacity of 100,000 tonnes of solar panels, includes robotic handling, feed conveyance, particle sizing, shredding and conditioning systems, alongside Comstock’s proprietary processing technology.\nComstock Metals has also installed and tested upgraded glass-processing systems ahead of its original production schedule. The additional capability is designed to enable production of higher-specification glass products.\n“While our recycling and upgrading operations ramp up, we have also advanced the development of our proprietary metals recovery processes. These novel metal extraction technologies should enable the economic recovery of silver and other metals and materials from our industrial offcut stream,” Villamagna added.\nThe company said it remains on schedule for its next major milestone: the development of a one-tonne-per-day pilot system.\nThe announcement comes as Comstock continues to ramp up its solar PV recycling footprint in the US. In July, it signed a recycling agreement with Illuminate USA for its 5GW Ohio module plant, while in June the firm partnered with JobsOhio and OhioSE on a recycling and logistics hub in Cambridge, Ohio.\nComstock also secured California certification to process PV modules in February and completed permitting for a Nevada facility in January, designed to process up to 3 million end-of-life modules annually.\nOnePlanet unveils automated solar recycling process targeting 2027 production\nIn other news, Jacksonville, Florida-headquartered solar recycling company OnePlanet has launched PRISM, a fully automated, mechanical process",
