     "summary": "More than 100 personnel have already relocated to Redstone Arsenal as construction of billion-dollar Space Command facility is set to begin The post Space Comma",
     "source": "SpaceNews",
     "content": "HUNTSVILLE, Ala. — Five years after a bruising political fight over where U.S. Space Command headquarters should be located, the debate is giving way to bulldozers, construction contracts and the practical challenges of moving one of the U.S. military’s most complex commands.\nAt Redstone Arsenal, where a 64-acre site has been cleared for U.S. Space Command’s future headquarters, Gen. Stephen Whiting now works from a temporary office roughly once every six weeks as he divides his time between Alabama and Colorado Springs, where the command continues to operate.\nThe arrangement will continue for years.\nIf the current schedule holds, roughly half of the headquarters staff will be based in Huntsville by 2028, with the permanent facility completed in 2031 and the full workforce of about 1,800 personnel moving into the building the following year.\n“We have been moving with speed and with enthusiasm to get established,” Whiting said during a news conference at the construction site.\nSince President Trump ordered the headquarters relocated to Huntsville last September, more than 100 Space Command personnel have begun working permanently at Redstone, most of them intelligence analysts based in a Defense Intelligence Agency facility on the installation. The command expects that number to reach 200 by the end of 2026 before accelerating the move as construction progresses on a new headquarters complex known as the Command and Control Facility, or C2F.\nThe Pentagon’s fiscal 2027 budget requests $951 million for the relocation, covering design, military construction, information technology and other aspects of the move. Space Command expects to announce a major construction-support contract soon, followed by a request for proposals for the company that will build the headquarters itself.\nWhiting said contracting authorities approved by Congress will allow the project to be managed more like a commercial development than a traditional Pentagon construction effort, with the goal of shortening schedules and reducing costs.\nThe relocation represents the latest chapter in a long-running political dispute.\nThe Air Force selected Redstone Arsenal in January 2021, during the final days of President Trump’s first administration, after a nationwide basing competition. Colorado officials immediately challenged the decision, arguing that Peterson Space Force Base, where the command was already operating, offered greater military value and avoided the cost and disruption of a move.\nThe Biden administration suspended implementation while multiple reviews were conducted by the Pentagon inspector general and the Government Accountability Office. In 2023, the administration announced the headquarters would remain in Colorado Springs, prompting Alabama lawmakers to accuse the White House of overriding the Air Force’s original selection for political reasons.\nThe issue remained unresolved until Trump returned to office and directed the Pentagon last September to proceed with th",
