     "summary": "Mike Fincke trained on the Boeing CST-100 Starliner side of the Commercial Crew Program from January 2019 until his reassignment to the SpaceX Crew-11 mission. ",
     "source": "Spaceflight Now",
     "content": "Mike Fincke, a retired United States Air Force colonel and NASA astronaut of 30 years, is retiring from the agency’s Astronaut Office. He’s one of only 13 people who launched to orbit from Earth onboard three different spacecraft.\nFincke among 44 astronauts selected in 1996 for the 16th astronaut class, nicknamed “The Sardines.” He flew to space four times: twice on Soyuz, once on Space Shuttle Endeavour, and once on the SpaceX Dragon Endeavour.\n“After exactly 30 years, I am departing NASA, but I remain deeply committed to the work of exploration.” Fincke said in a statement shared by the agency. “NASA gave me the extraordinary privilege of serving alongside remarkable people, flying and helping develop spacecraft, and contributing to the International Space Station from its earliest days through command in orbit.”\nFincke’s time as a NASA astronaut saw him serve as commander of the International Space Station twice. The first time was from Oct. 22, 2008 through April 2, 2009, during Expedition 18 and the second time was from Dec. 7, 2025 through Jan. 12, 2026, during Expedition 74.\n“I am profoundly grateful to my crewmates, the teams on the ground, our international partners, and the families who make this work possible. I am excited to carry those lessons forward and help prepare the next generation of engineers, explorers, and leaders,” Fincke said. “Together, we will return humanity to the Moon, travel to Mars, journey outward to the planets and moons beyond Earth, and someday reach for the stars – all while caring for Earth, the most beautiful planet in our solar system.”\nA notable chunk of Fincke’s NASA career was in service of the Commercial Crew Program. He was at one time the chief of the Astronaut Office’s Commercial Crew Branch and in January 2019, he replaced Eric Boe as one of three crew members slated to fly the Crewed Flight Test of the Boeing CST-100 Starliner spacecraft.\nAs development of that program dragged on and crews were shuffled multiple times, Fincke was moved to the backup pilot role on the CFT mission and eventually trained alongside NASA astronaut Scott Tingle, Canadian Space Agency (CSA) astronaut Joshua Kutryk, and Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency (JAXA) astronaut Kimiya Yui as a member of the Starliner-1 mission.\nHowever, after the ill-fated flight of the CFT mission in 2024 with NASA astronauts Butch Wilmore and Suni Williams, Fincke and Yui were reassigned to the SpaceX Crew-11 mission. The duo launched to the space station alongside NASA astronaut Zena Cardman and Roscosmos cosmonaut Oleg Platonov on Aug. 1, 2025, for a mission that lasted 166 days.\nBut the mission ended early when Fincke had to be medically evacuated from the ISS.\nInitially, NASA withheld the identity of the crew member that triggered the medical emergency, but Fincke later told the Associated Press he had a bizarre episode while eating dinner on Jan. 7 when he just suddenly couldn’t speak for about 20 minutes.\n“It was completely out of the blu",
