     "content": "Firefly Aerospace said it will be at least another two months before it’s ready to launch its next Alpha rocket. Alpha Flight 8 (FLTA008) will debut the Block 2 configuration of the rocket.\nDuring its second quarter earnings call on Tuesday, Firefly CEO Jason Kim said the next two Alpha launches are now expected to take place in the fourth quarter of 2026.\n“We are preparing for acceptance testing of Alpha in the next few weeks, followed by delivery to Vandenberg, static fire, payload integration and then launch,” Kim told investors. “As with any flight, we’ll disclose more specific timing as we get closer to launch.”\nFollowing a successful launch of Flight 7 on March 11, Kim said the goal is to close out the year with three Alpha missions. He said that the rockets for Flight 10 and Flight 11 are “flowing through our production line” and stated that they sold the majority of the company’s 2027 launch manifest.\nKim didn’t name a specific number of launches planned for next year.\n“We have visibility into out years as well, thank to the extension of our Lockheed Martin multi-launch agreement,” Kim said. “Additionally, we added a second hypersonic task order for Alpha from a confidential customer.”\nIn addition to its launch pad at Vandenberg Space Force Base, Firefly is also moving forward with SSC Space’s Esrange Space Center to bring Launch Complex 3C online with the first launch from that site planned in 2028.\nFirefly increased its research and development costs by $25.8 million for the second quarter of 2026 as compared to the same time period of 2025, according to a financial disclosure to the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission. The company said that it stemmed mainly from “costs related to the Alpha program associated with ramping production on our Alpha Block 2 configuration upgrade, stock-based compensation expense, depreciation and amortization from newly acquired assets being placed into service, and other R&D initiatives.”\nThe next evolution of Alpha.\nBlock II is the next-generation configuration of our Alpha rocket and the only operational U.S. 1-ton rocket. The striking white exterior isn’t just for looks—it enhances thermal protection and is one of several engineering upgrades across the… pic.twitter.com/FdYCQPj3gL\n— Firefly Aerospace (@Firefly_Space) August 11, 2026\nThe shift to the Alpha Block 2 rocket was necessitated by the in-flight anomaly seen during the Alpha Flight 6 mission on April 29, 2025. Teams determined that increased thermal protection system thickness on the booster was needed after a rupture occurred following stage separation.\nThe company also experienced an explosion of one of its Alpha boosters during an acceptance test at its facilities in Briggs, Texas, in late September.\nThe Alpha Block 2 rocket is designed to overcome the issues that led to that explosion. That includes increasing the length of the vehicle by seven feet to account for larger liquid oxygen tanks; shifting to in-house manufactured batteries a",
