     "title": "‘That is not acceptable’: Judge orders Google to make rival app store installs easier",
     "url": "https://www.theverge.com/policy/979852/that-is-not-acceptable-judge-orders-google-to-make-rival-app-store-installs-easier",
     "time": "08-14 18:01",
     "ts": 1786701695,
     "summary": "One month after Epic Games and Google seemingly stopped fighting over the future of Android app distribution, they were back in a San Francisco courtroom today ",
     "source": "The Verge",
     "content": "One month after Epic Games and Google seemingly stopped fighting over the future of Android app distribution, they were back in a San Francisco courtroom today — where Judge James Donato just ordered Google to make it easier to install rival app stores on Android.\n‘That is not acceptable’: Judge orders Google to make rival app store installs easier\nJudge Donato was not having Google’s ‘anticompetitive friction.’\nJudge Donato was not having Google’s ‘anticompetitive friction.’\nIt’s been nearly three years since a jury unanimously decided that Google had an illegal monopoly over Android apps, and almost two years since Judge Donato decided the best way of undoing that monopoly would be to crack open Android app distribution. Donato ordered Google to carry rival Android app stores inside its own Google Play Store, and to provide rivals with complete access to Google’s full catalog of apps, for several years.\nBut Epic argued that Google is still making it too difficult to install rival app stores, showing the entire courtroom a live demo of how many steps it currently takes — and Judge Donato agreed that some of those steps were unnecessary “anticompetitive friction” and ordered Google to remove them.\nFirst, Epic’s lawyer Yonatan Even showed that if you search Google’s Play Store for the term “store for apps,” no third-party app stores appear, only physical stores. “Wait, why did Walmart come up?” Judge Donato asked. “That’s not good,” he exclaimed.\n“That is not acceptable, that has to be fixed. I want every possible variation that’s even only 70 percent properly phrased,” Donato ordered. Google’s counsel agreed to make it happen.\nSimilarly, Judge Donato bristled when he saw that users have to press a “view” button before they can press an “install” button when choosing to install a third-party app store, and so that will change too. “You don’t get that when you’re looking at Apple Music,” he noted.\n“Make that install, not view,” Donato ordered, and Google agreed to do so.\nLater, Epic showed that when you search for “app store,” or even if you search directly for Aptoide, the first rival store to make it into the Google Play Store, Google doesn’t surface a normal list of apps — it shows you a new banner you have to click through to a dedicated third-party app store page.\nThat’s not just a problem for brand-new app stores, argued Even. Big, established companies like Amazon might decide not to launch app stores because they’d be losing the ability to appear in normal app store search results.\nJudge Donato ordered Google to fix that one, too, telling the company that it didn’t make any sense. “Why doesn’t it say ‘are you looking for music’ when you type in ‘music store’?” he asked.\nGoogle’s attorney replied that Epic and Google had already agreed that app stores are a special category of apps that have advanced permissions. But Donato said the warning on the third-party app store page itself was sufficient to address that — there’s no need to ask a use",
     "_extract_method": "trafilatura",
     "zh": "“这不可接受”：法官下令谷歌简化第三方应用商店安装流程",
     "content_zh": "摘要：Epic Games与谷歌就安卓应用分发争议停战一个月后，双方于旧金山法庭再次对峙。法官詹姆斯·多纳特指出谷歌设置的安装步骤包含不必要的“反竞争阻碍”，下令其简化第三方应用商店安装流程。此前陪审团已一致裁定谷歌非法垄断安卓应用市场，多纳特此前已判决要求谷歌在Play商店内置第三方应用商店、向竞争对手开放全量应用目录数年，但Epic现场演示指出谷歌仍刻意提高安装门槛，多纳特认可该主张并作出上述命令。"
