     "title": "Google ordered to remove scary warnings when installing third-party app stores",
     "url": "https://www.androidauthority.com/google-play-store-remove-third-party-app-store-friction-3698697/",
     "time": "08-14 17:03",
     "ts": 1786698217,
     "summary": "US Judge rules that Play Store's warning screens are anticompetitive friction that must end.",
     "source": "Android Authority",
     "content": "Affiliate links on Android Authority may earn us a commission. Learn more.\nGoogle ordered to remove scary warnings when installing third-party app stores\nAug 14, 2026 — 5:03 AM ET\n- US District Judge James Donato has ordered Google to eliminate extra steps and warning prompts when users install rival Android app stores through the Play Store.\n- The process for installing rival Android app stores involves multiple steps, such as tapping a “view” button before an “install” button appears.\n- The court called these steps deliberate “anticompetitive friction” designed to scare off everyday users.\nUS District Judge James Donato has ordered Google to streamline the installation process for competing Android app stores, rebuking the company for inserting unnecessary roadblocks into the user experience. The order lands just as Google is being forced to open its ecosystem under antitrust pressure, a shift highlighted by third-party storefronts like Aptoide Games launching directly on the Play Store.\nDuring a recent compliance hearing in the long-running Epic vs Google antitrust battle, Judge Donato took direct aim at the multi-step hurdles Google created for alternative app stores, declaring, “That is not acceptable,” as reported by The Verge.\nEpic’s legal team demonstrated that users are subjected to superfluous prompts and several warning and confirmation screens, including a requirement to tap a “view” button before an “install” option is even presented.\nThe court slammed this as deliberate “anticompetitive friction” intended to scare off everyday consumers. The judge subsequently ordered Google to remove these intermediate barriers and make installing an alternative marketplace as straightforward as installing any other Android application, with changes to be made within a week.\nWhile Android has technically allowed sideloading and third-party stores for over a decade, Google has persistently surrounded the process with ominous security warnings, buried permission toggles, and multi-step dialogs that make sideloading feel hazardous to non-technical users.\nThis directive builds directly on the remedies stemming from Epic Games’ landmark antitrust victory, in which a jury found that Google held an illegal monopoly over Android app distribution and in-app billing services. While Google continues to argue that these friction points are critical for user security and device safety, the court has made it clear that safety cannot double as a convenient shield to preserve market dominance.\nThank you for being part of our community. Read our Comment Policy before posting.",
     "_extract_method": "trafilatura",
     "zh": "谷歌被要求移除安装第三方应用商店时的恐吓式警告",
     "content_zh": "2026年8月14日消息，美国地区法官詹姆斯·多纳托在Epic诉谷歌反垄断案合规听证中下令，要求谷歌取消用户在Play商店安装第三方安卓应用商店时的额外步骤和警告提示，法院认定这些多步骤设置是谷歌刻意设置的“反竞争阻碍”，意图吓退普通用户。该裁决出台时谷歌正因反垄断压力被迫开放其生态，已有Aptoide Games等第三方商店直接在Play商店上架。"
