     "title": "Apple seeks settlement talks with Epic Games in new court filing",
     "url": "https://9to5mac.com/2026/08/13/apple-seeks-settlement-talks-with-epic-games-in-new-court-filing/",
     "time": "08-14 09:39",
     "ts": 1786671547,
     "summary": "In addition to submitting its proposed commission structure for purchases made outside the App Store’s IAP system in the US, Apple filed a motion indicating it ",
     "source": "9to5Mac",
     "content": "In addition to submitting its proposed commission structure for purchases made outside the App Store’s IAP system in the US, Apple filed a motion indicating it is willing to explore a settlement with Epic Games. Here are the details.\nApple might be ready to settle\nEarlier today, Apple submitted its proposal outlining the commission it wants to be allowed to charge on purchases completed through payment systems outside the App Store.\nThe filing came just as the Supreme Court rejected Apple’s request to pause the lower-court proceedings while it reviews Judge Yvonne Gonzalez Rogers’ contempt finding over the company’s decision to charge a 27% commission after being ordered to allow developers to direct users to outside payment methods.\nUnder Apple’s proposed structure, the company could charge commissions of up to 15% on purchases completed through alternative payment systems. Epic Games immediately pushed back, saying it “believes these fees are far outside of the bounds of the Ninth Circuit’s guidance on permissible fees.”\nAnd while that filing has gotten most of the attention, Apple also submitted another motion today asking Judge Gonzalez Rogers to order the parties into a settlement conference.\nFrom the filing (edited for clarity):\n[Apple] respectfully moves the Court for an order referring the parties to a settlement conference before Magistrate Judge Joseph C. Spero. Counsel for Apple conferred with counsel for [Epic] on August 11, 2026 regarding the requested relief. […] Epic did not consent to the relief sought herein.\nThis means that Apple’s legal team approached Epic’s lawyers about seeking a settlement conference before filing the motion, but Epic did not agree to Apple’s request.\nIn the filing, Apple argues that the court has the authority to order the parties into a settlement conference even without Epic’s consent, and adds that the Ninth Circuit itself encouraged both sides to reach an agreement on an appropriate link-out commission.\nThe company also says that “discussions of a potential settlement in a confidential, non-adversarial setting would increase the likelihood of reaching a practical resolution that could obviate the need for prolonged remand proceedings.”\nIt is worth noting that the filing does not contain Apple’s proposals or offers for a settlement, nor does it constitute a settlement proposal per se. Rather, Apple is asking the court to order the parties into settlement talks under the supervision of Magistrate Judge Joseph C. Spero.\nYou can read Apple’s full petition below:\nWhat terms do you think Apple is going to propose? Let us know in the comments.\nWorth checking out on Amazon\n- Geoffrey Cain – ‘Steve Jobs in Exile’\n- David Pogue – ’Apple: The First 50 Years’\n- MacBook Neo\n- Logitech MX Master 4\n- AirPods Pro 3\n- AirTag (2nd Generation) – 4 Pack\n- Apple Watch Series 11\n- Wireless CarPlay adapter\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.",
     "_extract_method": "trafilatura",
     "zh": "苹果在新提交文件中寻求与Epic Games和解",
     "content_zh": "苹果在向美国法院提交的佣金方案文件中表示愿意与Epic Games探讨和解。此前最高法院驳回苹果暂停审理下级法院裁定的请求，该裁定与苹果被要求允许开发者引导用户使用外部支付方式后，仍收取27%佣金一事有关。苹果提出的方案为，对通过App Store外支付系统完成的购买，最高可收取15%佣金，Epic Games随即回应称该费率远超相关司法指导范围。此外苹果还提交动议，请求法官命令双方启动和解谈判。"
