     "title": "Honor launches its Robot Phone with a built-in folding gimbal and some crazy promo videos",
     "url": "https://www.dpreview.com/news/honor-launches-its-robot-phone-with-a-built-in-folding-gimbal/",
     "time": "08-13 13:57",
     "ts": 1786600673,
     "summary": "It's currently only available in China, but could be an interesting idea for the future of mobile videography",
     "source": "DPReview",
     "content": "When Honor first showed off the Robot Phone last year, it seemed like an idea that would forever live as just a concept and CGI renders. A phone with a built-in gimbal that folds into the camera bump? That’s also a collaboration with cinema camera company Arri? It all seemed a bit far fetched.\nAnd yet today, the company actually launched it, and it’s for sale in China. At least a few members of the press have even had the opportunity to try it, and Honor teamed up with cinematographers to film some truly wild promo videos with it to show off its video capabilities.\nI, personally, would not trust the AI assistant in my phone to control my spaceship.\nThe Robot Phone’s main camera – the one mounted on the integrated three-axis gimbal – has a 200MP Type 1/1.28 (76mm2) sensor with a 23mm equiv. F1.6 lens. It’s optically stabilized, but obviously the titanium gimbal will do most of the heavy lifting in that regard.\nIt’s not just for keeping the camera steady during longer exposures or video though; there’s an AI tracking mode to keep your subject centered in the frame, even as you move around, a panorama mode, a star assist feature to track stars across the sky for astrophotography and time lapses, and a rotation mode that will produce a dramatic spinning shot. There are also several different stabilization modes for the gimbal, depending on the look you’re going for.",
     "_extract_method": "trafilatura",
