     "title": "These mammal ancestors birthed live young far earlier than thought",
     "url": "https://www.sciencenews.org/article/mammal-ancestors-birthed-live-earlier",
     "time": "08-13 12:00",
     "ts": 1786593600,
     "summary": "Growth rings on a fossil bone suggest at least one mammal ancestor gave birth to live young rather than laying eggs 236 million years ago.",
     "source": "Science News",
     "content": "These mammal ancestors birthed live young far earlier than thought\nNew fossil pushes a shift from egg laying to live birth back by 90 million years\nMammal ancestors may have made the evolutionary leap from egg-laying to live birth at least 90 million years earlier than thought.\nGrowth rings within fossil bones from an adult Chiniquodon theotonicus, a species of cynodont that lived around 236 million years ago, reveal that the animal was relatively large when it was a baby, researchers report August 13 in Frontiers in Mammal Science. Such hefty newborns, relative to adult body size, are typical of modern mammals, but not of egg hatchlings, whether reptiles or birds. That, the team says, suggests that this cynodont fossil could be the earliest evidence of live birth.\nIt’s long been a mystery when mammalian ancestors became viviparous, giving birth to live young, says Leandro Gaetano, a paleontologist at the National Scientific and Technical Research Council in Buenos Aires. Mammals are the only surviving lineage of the cynodonts (meaning “dog teeth”), a group of creatures that appeared around 260 million years ago, toward the end of the Permian Period.\nCynodonts had some powerful evolutionary advances relative to their own ancestors, the therapsids. They had differentiated teeth, sporting canines, incisors and molars, which made it possible to process a variety of foods through cutting, tearing and grinding. Their jaws were adapted for chewing, including a secondary palate that allowed them to chew and breathe at the same time, speeding up digestion and making it possible to fuel a higher metabolic rate.\nAs a result, cynodonts were at least partially warm-blooded. That, along with their burrowing abilities, may have helped them survive the mass extinction at the end of the Permian, around 252 million years ago.\nLive birth is a characteristic of most modern mammals, with the exception of egg-laying monotremes like platypuses. But viviparity wasn’t thought to appear until much later in the cynodont lineage, with the emergence of a group called the therian mammals around 160 million years ago.\nThe new timeline comes from analyses of a C. theotonicus specimen found in northwestern Argentina. The team examined growth rings in the creature’s bones and identified a neonatal line, a microscopic dark ring that marks the intense metabolic stress of being born, separating prenatal tissue from postnatal tissue.\nRinging up evidence\nThis cross section of the microscopic bone structure of a femur of Chiniquodon theotonicus shows evidence the animal was born live rather than hatching from an egg. The darker purple line curving from left to right, known as a neonatal line, marks a shift from embryonic tissue (below the line) to post-birth tissue, which contains a denser network of blood vessels (above).\nUsing the neonatal line and other bone-growth rings, the team estimated that the animal weighed about 1.7 kilograms at birth and about 12 kilograms when it died. Th",
